Android Application Reverse Engineering Methodologies

Part 8 — Native Android (NDK-Heavy) Application Reverse Engineering Methodology (Beginner → Expert)

> Goal: Learn how to systematically understand Android applications whose primary logic resides in native code written in C or C++. These applications rely heavily on the Android NDK and execute much of their behavior inside ELF shared libraries rather than DEX. By the end of this section, you should be able to reconstruct native architecture, JNI integration, memory layout, threading, library interactions, and the relationship between native code and the Android framework.



> Core Principle:
In native-heavy applications, the APK is only the entry point. The real application often lives inside one or more native libraries.




