Stage 30 — Common Native Investigation Mistakes

Avoid

Treating native code as an extension of Java instead of a separate application.

Ignoring library initialization order.

Confusing compiler-generated code with handwritten logic.

Following individual functions without reconstructing subsystems.

Ignoring ownership and memory lifetime.

Focusing only on JNI while neglecting native architecture.

Assuming one native library contains all business logic.



---

Stage 31 — Native APK Methodology Master Checklist

By the end of this stage, you should be able to:

✅ Identify and profile native-heavy Android applications.

✅ Reconstruct ELF library organization, dependencies, and initialization.

✅ Recover JNI boundaries, native object models, and subsystem architecture.

✅ Analyze memory layout, ownership, threading, synchronization, and persistence at an architectural level.

✅ Trace interactions between native code, Java/Kotlin, and the Android framework.

✅ Distinguish compiler/runtime infrastructure from handwritten native logic.

✅ Validate reconstructed architecture through runtime observation.

✅ Produce comprehensive documentation describing the native application's architecture, execution model, and Android integration.



